Looking at this problem:

**Key insight:** Each `1×1` cell can be split into 4 triangles (Top, Right, Bottom, Left) meeting at the center. A `/` divides the cell into the upper-left half ({Top, Left}) and lower-right half ({Right, Bottom}); a `\` divides it into upper-right ({Top, Right}) and lower-left ({Bottom, Left}); a space keeps all 4 together. Adjacent cells connect across shared edges (Right of one cell with Left of the next, Bottom with Top of the cell below). Regions touching only at a point do **not** merge (confirmed by example 3 giving 5).

Using Union-Find over `4n²` nodes, the answer is the number of connected components.

I verified this against all three examples:
- `[" /", "/ "]` → 2 ✓
- `[" /", "  "]` → 1 ✓  
- `["/\", "\/"]` → 5 ✓ (4 corner triangles + center diamond)

Also cross-checked trickier cases (X-pattern `["\/", "/\"]` → 4, and `["//", "\ "]` → 3, where two half-cells connect around a free line endpoint) against a brute-force 3×3-upscale flood fill — both agree.

